WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.780
More than 20 million flex-fuel vehicles earned fuel credits in 2014, while almost never

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:13.940
burning E85. This is unintended consequences, episode 143. Those vehicles sat in American

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:19.320
driveways with a special credit attached to each one. The credit let a manufacturer meet

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:24.700
fuel economy rules without a single gallon of E85 ever leaving a pump into that tank.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:30.560
The Alternative Motorfuels Act of 1988 had offered the credits to push ethanol into

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:36.560
the market and cut oil imports. What followed was different. The credits mainly helped Detroit

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:43.430
keep selling larger gasoline engines. E85 pumps stayed rare outside the Midwest. The gap

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:48.850
between a certified vehicle and a filled tank is the whole story. Congress wrote the Alternative

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:54.730
Motorfuels Act in 1988. The oil shocks of the 1970s were still fresh in memory.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:59.250
The farm crisis of the 1980s was still unfolding in corn country.

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:01.830
Lawmakers wanted two things at once.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.750
Lawmakers wanted to stretch gasoline supplies.

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:09.050
Lawmakers also wanted to give corn growers a new outlet for the harvest.

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:14.390
The act let automakers count each flex fuel vehicle as far more efficient than the vehicle

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:15.190
actually was.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:21.190
The method was a 0.15 divisor applied to the alternative fuel portion of the vehicle's

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:21.610
performance.

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:25.690
At the time, that arithmetic looked like a straightforward reward.

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:31.210
Build a vehicle that could run on ethanol blends up to E85 and the fleet average would

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:31.570
move.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:36.450
Policy makers expected rising ethanol use to follow the vehicles onto the road.

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:40.930
The vehicles would appear, the fuel would find them, and oil imports would ease.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:43.270
The formula had a concrete shape.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:49.590
If a vehicle achieved 15 miles per gallon on E85, the formula multiplied that figure by

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:56.370
roughly 6.67. That boosted number was then averaged with the gasoline only figure. The result

00:01:56.370 --> 00:02:01.650
was a large lift in the manufacturer's overall fleet average. The credit was not unlimited.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:08.010
The cap sat at 1.2 miles per gallon per fleet. In absolute terms, the incentive looked modest.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:12.470
Standards, though, were tightening by a few tenths of a mile per gallon each year.

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:17.250
Against that slow climb, 1.2 miles per gallon was large enough to matter.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:21.710
Here, rational legislators therefore treated the divisor as a low-cost lever.

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:28.110
The lever seemed to align three goals, energy security, agricultural support, emissions

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.210
reduction, and the lever did not require new taxes.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:35.010
The lever also did not require direct spending on fuel stations.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.250
That last choice felt like restraint, not neglect.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:41.950
If the vehicles were capable, the market would supply the fuel.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:48.070
Congress was rewarding a capability, and capability looked like the hard part. Building a car that

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:53.510
can drink a new fuel is an engineering problem with a finish line. Standing up a national retail

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:59.330
network is a different kind of problem, slower and less visible from a hearing room. The people who

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:04.330
wrote the act were working with the shocks and the farm crisis in front of them. A credit inside

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:10.230
the existing fuel economy system was a tool already on the table. The corporate average fuel economy

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:16.110
rules, cafe, where where manufacturers already had to show their work. Folding ethanol into

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:21.130
cafe meant the incentive would show up where companies already made decisions. That was the

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:27.410
appeal, no new agency, no new appropriation for pumps, a line in a formula, and three problems

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:33.363
addressed at once. Automakers responded quickly once the credits appeared in the cafe calculations.

00:03:33.943 --> 00:03:40.323
By the mid-1990s, Ford, General Motors, and Chrysler offered flex-fuel versions of popular trucks

00:03:40.323 --> 00:03:46.363
and sport utility vehicles. The 1.2-mile cap was still the ceiling. That margin proved

00:03:46.363 --> 00:03:51.683
valuable when meeting tightening standards. A few tens of compliance, bought with a fuel system

00:03:51.683 --> 00:03:57.343
change rather than a smaller engine, was a rational trade for a product planner. Early fleet

00:03:57.343 --> 00:04:02.363
data showed steady growth in the number of certified models. Proponents pointed to the rising

00:04:02.363 --> 00:04:08.483
count of dual fuel vehicles as proof the policy worked. The proof was the count. If more vehicles

00:04:08.483 --> 00:04:11.823
could use the fuel, the policy was doing what Congress had asked.

00:04:12.383 --> 00:04:14.123
A few analysts noted something else.

00:04:14.683 --> 00:04:18.403
E85 pumps were still concentrated in only a handful of states.

00:04:18.923 --> 00:04:21.783
The credits kept flowing regardless of actual fuel use.

00:04:22.283 --> 00:04:26.503
Nothing in the rule required a match between a certified vehicle and a nearby pump.

00:04:27.143 --> 00:04:30.683
The implementation relied on the existing gasoline distribution network.

00:04:31.483 --> 00:04:33.643
Manufacturers did not have to build stations.

00:04:34.283 --> 00:04:36.963
Manufacturers added corrosion-resistant fuel lines,

00:04:36.963 --> 00:04:40.983
engines, modified injectors, and a sensor that detected ethanol content.

00:04:41.443 --> 00:04:44.543
Those changes added only a few hundred dollars per vehicle.

00:04:45.023 --> 00:04:49.403
A few hundred dollars is a small line on a truck that already carries a large sticker.

00:04:50.043 --> 00:04:54.763
Because the regulatory credit did not require proof that any particular vehicle ever used

00:04:54.763 --> 00:04:55.483
E85.

00:04:56.163 --> 00:04:59.203
The same hardware could go on high-volume truck platforms.

00:04:59.843 --> 00:05:02.003
Dealer inventories did not have to change.

00:05:02.463 --> 00:05:04.523
Consumer behavior did not have to change.

00:05:04.523 --> 00:05:10.358
A buyer could leave the lot in a flex fuel truck and never learn where E85 was sold.

00:05:10.878 --> 00:05:14.458
The truck would run on ordinary gasoline and the credit would still count.

00:05:15.078 --> 00:05:19.758
Early success metrics therefore tracked production numbers rather than fuel throughput at pumps.

00:05:20.518 --> 00:05:23.578
That measurement choice was quiet and it decided the rest.

00:05:24.078 --> 00:05:27.018
A factory can count vehicles as the vehicles leave the line.

00:05:27.618 --> 00:05:29.918
A pump can only count gallons if someone stops.

00:05:29.918 --> 00:05:36.298
The rule asked for the first number. Detroit supplied the first number. Ford, General Motors,

00:05:36.598 --> 00:05:40.878
and Chrysler were not ignoring the statute. The companies were meeting the statute as

00:05:40.878 --> 00:05:46.058
written. A flex fuel truck in the lineup was a compliant truck. The corrosion-resistant

00:05:46.058 --> 00:05:51.498
lines and the ethanol sensor were real engineering. The vehicles could, in fact, run the blend.

00:05:52.078 --> 00:05:57.178
What the vehicles could do and what drivers did were treated as the same fact. The analysts

00:05:57.178 --> 00:06:00.258
lists who watched the handful of states were watching a different fact.

00:06:00.738 --> 00:06:03.978
Station maps and certification lists were telling two stories.

00:06:04.638 --> 00:06:05.658
The lists kept growing.

00:06:06.038 --> 00:06:06.838
The maps did not.

00:06:07.398 --> 00:06:10.778
The maps did not, credits did not wait for the maps to catch up.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:16.200
By the time the fleet had grown into the tens of millions, the alternative fuels data

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:20.600
center counted just over 4,000 public E85 stations nationwide.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:25.600
The governor's biofuels coalition was the source for the vehicle count that opened this

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:25.960
story.

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:29.140
Most of those stations sat in corn belt states.

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:32.080
Drivers almost never filled up with E85.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:34.200
The stations were too far apart.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:36.620
The fuel often cost more per mile.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:41.920
A driver doing ordinary errands does not detour across a state for a fuel that costs more

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:43.220
to travel the same distance.

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:48.760
Manufacturers therefore treated the vehicles as gasoline only for real world planning.

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:54.760
The same manufacturers use the regulatory credits to offset larger, thirstier engines in the

00:06:54.760 --> 00:07:00.120
same lineup. That is the causal chain, and it is worth walking slowly. The credit

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:05.400
improved the fleet average on paper. A better paper average created room under the standard.

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:10.600
Room under the standard could be spent on a larger gasoline engine that buyers wanted,

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:15.720
and that the showroom already knew how to sell. The flex fuel hardware made the room.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:21.560
The larger engine used the room, the result was slower improvement in overall fleet efficiency

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:26.920
than the raw cafe numbers suggested. The numbers were not false. The numbers described

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:33.260
certified capability, not fuel burned. Ethanol demand grew far more slowly than the 1988

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:39.680
projections had assumed. Corn growers saw modest new markets. The hoped for broad shift away

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:46.180
from gasoline did not occur. Second order effects appeared in station economics. Without steady volume,

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:51.500
fuel retailers outside the Midwest had little reason to install E85 tanks and pumps.

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:54.180
A pump is a bet on repeat customers.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:08:00.260
If the trucks in the parking lot are certified for E85 and filled with gasoline, the bet does

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:00.720
not pay.

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:03.740
The chicken and egg problem was self-reinforcing.

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:08.760
A driver who considered buying a flex fuel truck could calculate that the nearest E85

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:12.400
station lay beyond the vehicle's comfortable range on a single tank.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:15.800
So the truck was operated exclusively on gasoline.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:19.280
Exclusive gasoline use meant no volume at the pump.

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:25.160
pump. No volume meant no new pumps. No new pumps meant the next driver made the same calculation.

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:31.180
Third order effects showed up in later cafe rule makings. Regulators had to tighten the very

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:36.500
credits that had once seemed generous. Tightening was not a simple correction. The credits had

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:41.700
already been banked against larger engine families. Any sudden removal would have forced abrupt

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:47.340
redesigns of best-selling pickups and sport utility vehicles. Those vehicles were already in

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:50.440
production plans, dealer orders, and family driveways.

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:54.700
A credit that had looked like a low-cost lever had become part of the product.

00:08:55.693 --> 00:09:00.873
The policy therefore locked in a fleet composition that continued to favor gasoline power trains,

00:09:01.293 --> 00:09:03.533
even after the statutory phase-out began.

00:09:04.393 --> 00:09:06.833
Lock-in is not a scandal in the ordinary sense.

00:09:07.613 --> 00:09:11.553
Lock-in is what happens when a measurement rewards the vehicle and the vehicle then

00:09:11.553 --> 00:09:13.053
shapes the engines around it.

00:09:13.053 --> 00:09:18.753
Detroit planned real-world operation as gasoline only because that is how the trucks were driven.

00:09:19.333 --> 00:09:21.773
The larger engines were not a secret side door.

00:09:22.313 --> 00:09:25.933
The larger engines were the use of a credit the statute had made available.

00:09:26.673 --> 00:09:30.413
Buyers for their part were not refusing a fuel that sat across the street.

00:09:30.933 --> 00:09:34.213
The fuel was far and the fuel often cost more per mile.

00:09:34.733 --> 00:09:37.893
Distance and price are ordinary reasons to stay with gasoline.

00:09:37.893 --> 00:09:42.333
The Midwest concentration of stations made the ordinary reason national.

00:09:42.973 --> 00:09:47.093
Outside the corn belt, a flex-fuel badge was a capability without a neighborhood.

00:09:47.753 --> 00:09:53.693
Inside the corn belt, stations existed, and even there the cost per mile comparison often favored gasoline.

00:09:54.573 --> 00:09:56.473
Modest new markets for corn were real.

00:09:57.153 --> 00:10:00.573
Modest is not the shift the 1988 projections had described.

00:10:01.253 --> 00:10:04.973
Projections had assumed that vehicles on the road would pull ethanol through the pumps.

00:10:04.973 --> 00:10:10.553
The pumps never became common enough to pull. Efficiency on the cafe books moved.

00:10:11.033 --> 00:10:16.633
Efficiency in the fuel actually burned, moved more slowly. That gap is the unintended consequence,

00:10:16.973 --> 00:10:22.053
and it compounded. Each year of credits banked against thirstier engines made the next year's

00:10:22.053 --> 00:10:27.653
lineup harder to change quickly. Regulators who later wanted a tighter rule were looking at factories

00:10:27.653 --> 00:10:33.113
already tooled for the engines the old credit had sheltered. Abrupt redesigns of best-selling

00:10:33.113 --> 00:10:38.873
pickups and sport utility vehicles are not a paper exercise. Suppliers, plants, and buyers

00:10:38.873 --> 00:10:43.953
are arranged around those vehicles. The phase out when it came had to live with that arrangement.

00:10:45.032 --> 00:10:49.712
The credits phased out after model year 2019 under the original statutory schedule.

00:10:50.292 --> 00:10:54.772
The schedule had been there from the start. What the schedule could not undo was the fleet

00:10:54.772 --> 00:11:00.632
already built. By 2022, only a handful of flex-fuel models remained in dealer showrooms.

00:11:00.632 --> 00:11:05.732
The Department of Energy's alternative fuels data center recorded roughly 21 million

00:11:05.732 --> 00:11:07.712
flex fuel vehicles still on the road.

00:11:08.112 --> 00:11:13.392
The number of new E85 stations grew only modestly to about 4,300.

00:11:13.992 --> 00:11:16.472
Some Midwest states kept investing in infrastructure.

00:11:17.292 --> 00:11:18.792
National momentum had faded.

00:11:19.332 --> 00:11:24.932
The 2020 technical correction from the Environmental Protection Agency clarified how future dual-fuel

00:11:24.932 --> 00:11:26.172
vehicles would be counted.

00:11:26.732 --> 00:11:29.272
That correction removed the old incentive structure.

00:11:29.272 --> 00:11:34.452
no new wave of flex-fuel introductions followed. The remaining vehicles on the road

00:11:34.452 --> 00:11:39.312
continued to operate almost entirely on E10 blends. The original goal of

00:11:39.312 --> 00:11:43.552
displacing large volumes of gasoline with higher ethanol blends stayed unmet.

00:11:44.192 --> 00:11:47.912
Infrastructure programs that did appear later focused on electric charging

00:11:47.912 --> 00:11:52.212
rather than liquid fuel pumps. Policy attention had shifted to a different

00:11:52.212 --> 00:11:57.252
technology pathway. The phase out was orderly in the statute and incomplete in the

00:11:57.252 --> 00:12:03.012
driveway. A handful of models in the showroom is a market that has moved on. 21 million

00:12:03.012 --> 00:12:07.992
vehicles still registered is a market that has not yet turned over. Those vehicles kept

00:12:07.992 --> 00:12:13.412
running on E10, the ordinary gasoline blend, because that is what the station sold everywhere.

00:12:14.032 --> 00:12:21.072
4,300 E85 stations after decades was growth, and it was modest growth. Midwest investment

00:12:21.072 --> 00:12:26.089
did not become a national network. Once the Environmental Protection Agency removed

00:12:26.089 --> 00:12:31.689
the old counting method. Manufacturers had no remaining reason to add the sensor and the lines for

00:12:31.689 --> 00:12:36.749
the credit alone. Introductions stopped. The hardware already on the road did not disappear.

00:12:37.389 --> 00:12:43.469
The hardware also did not start a late rush to E85. Capability without convenient fuel behaves

00:12:43.469 --> 00:12:49.209
like ordinary gasoline capability. Charging programs that came later were a different bet on a

00:12:49.209 --> 00:12:54.869
different fuel with a different kind of station. The shift in attention did not refill the E85

00:12:54.869 --> 00:12:58.809
tanks that had never been installed. It marked the end of the idea that a

00:12:58.809 --> 00:13:04.029
vehicle credit by itself would call a liquid fuel network into being. When Congress

00:13:04.029 --> 00:13:09.209
wrote the 1988 credits, Congress rewarded the act of certifying a vehicle rather

00:13:09.209 --> 00:13:13.649
than the act of delivering alternative fuel at scale. Automakers therefore

00:13:13.649 --> 00:13:18.289
optimized for certification volume, not for the network that would make E85

00:13:18.289 --> 00:13:23.329
convenient. That optimization was competent. A company measured on vehicle

00:13:23.329 --> 00:13:28.529
certified will build vehicles that certify. A company is not measured on gallons pumped

00:13:28.529 --> 00:13:33.729
if the rule never asks for gallons. Later regulators learn that infrastructure must be

00:13:33.729 --> 00:13:39.409
addressed at the same time as vehicle design if the fuel shift is to happen. Vehicle design

00:13:39.409 --> 00:13:45.009
and station design run on different clocks. A fuel system change can reach a showroom in a model

00:13:45.009 --> 00:13:51.169
year. A retail network needs volume before retailers will spend and volume needs the network before

00:13:51.169 --> 00:13:56.409
drivers will switch. Writing only the vehicle half of that pair leaves the other half to hope.

00:13:56.969 --> 00:14:01.909
Hope was the part that did not arrive. The same pattern appears today when credits are offered

00:14:01.909 --> 00:14:06.369
for vehicles that rely on charging or hydrogen networks that are still thin on the ground.

00:14:07.411 --> 00:14:12.671
A certified electric vehicle or a certified hydrogen vehicle can improve a paper average the way

00:14:12.671 --> 00:14:18.051
a flex fuel truck once did. If the credit does not ask whether energy actually move through

00:14:18.051 --> 00:14:23.051
the scarce network, the credit can again shelter the power trains that do not need that network.

00:14:23.951 --> 00:14:26.591
Environmental intelligence covers the regulatory side.

00:14:27.171 --> 00:14:31.371
How might today's efficiency or emissions rules change if the rules measured actual

00:14:31.371 --> 00:14:34.191
energy delivered instead of vehicles placed in service?

00:14:34.891 --> 00:14:36.071
That's the case for today.

00:14:36.651 --> 00:14:40.991
The lessons tend to travel further when you share them, so send this one to someone who'd

00:14:40.991 --> 00:14:43.871
find it useful, and subscribe wherever you're listening.

00:14:44.271 --> 00:14:48.031
See you tomorrow, and if you'd rather watch than listen, find us on YouTube.

